# PDF Reranker (Qwen3-Reranker, 4-bit)

A small Gradio app that ranks a set of PDFs by how relevant they are to a text query. It uses [Qwen3-Reranker](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-4B) loaded in 4-bit, and it reads only the **first few pages** of each PDF, so ranking stays fast even for long documents.

Upload some PDFs, type what you are looking for, and get a ranked table with a relevance score, the file name and a short text preview.

## Features

- Upload many PDFs at once and rank them against one query
- Relevance score between 0 and 1 for every PDF
- Runs the reranker in 4-bit (NF4) with `bitsandbytes`, so the 4B model fits on a modest GPU
- Editable task instruction (Advanced section) to steer the reranker
- Adjustable "Top N" number of results
- PDFs without extractable text are reported as skipped instead of failing the whole run

## How it works

1. **Extract text.** PyMuPDF reads the first `NUM_PAGES` pages (default 2) of each PDF and cleans the whitespace.
2. **Build a prompt.** Each PDF becomes one `(instruction, query, document)` input in the format Qwen3-Reranker expects. It is truncated to `MAX_LENGTH` tokens (default 3072).
3. **Score.** The model is asked whether the document meets the query. The app reads the logits of the answers "yes" and "no" at the last position and turns them into a probability of "yes". That probability is the score.
4. **Rank.** PDFs are sorted by score, highest first, and the top N are shown.

To reduce padding waste, documents are sorted by length before batching, and the scores are put back in the original order afterwards.

## Requirements

- Python 3.10 or newer
- A CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU (4-bit loading with `bitsandbytes` is designed for GPUs)
- Internet access on the first run, to download the model from Hugging Face

Python packages (see `requirements.txt`):

```
torch
transformers>=4.51
accelerate
bitsandbytes
gradio
pymupdf
```

## Installation

```bash
git clone <your-repo-url>
cd <your-repo-folder>

python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate

pip install -r requirements.txt
```

If `torch` installs a CPU-only build on your machine, install the CUDA build that matches your driver from the [PyTorch website](https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/) first, then run the command above.

## Usage

```bash
python app.py
```

The model is loaded once at start-up (the first run downloads it, so it takes a while). When the app is ready, Gradio prints a local address, usually `http://127.0.0.1:7860`. Open it in your browser, then:

1. Upload one or more PDFs.
2. Enter your query.
3. Choose how many results you want (Top N).
4. Optionally open **Advanced** and change the task instruction.
5. Click **Rerank**.

The result table has four columns: **Rank**, **Score**, **File** and **Text preview** (the first 400 characters of the text that was used). A status line above the table says how many PDFs were ranked and lists any that were skipped.

## Configuration

All settings are constants at the top of `app.py`:

| Setting | Default | What it does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `MODEL_ID` | `Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-4B` | Model to load. `Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-0.6B` is lighter and `Qwen/Qwen3-Reranker-8B` is larger. |
| `NUM_PAGES` | `2` | How many pages from the start of each PDF are used. |
| `MAX_LENGTH` | `3072` | Maximum number of tokens for each query + document pair. Longer text is truncated. |
| `BATCH_SIZE` | `1` | Pairs scored at once. Lower it (or keep it at 1) if you run out of GPU memory, raise it if you have spare memory. |
| `DEFAULT_INSTRUCTION` | `Given a web search query, retrieve relevant passages that answer the query` | Default task instruction. It can also be changed in the UI. |

Tip: a task-specific instruction often helps. For example, `Given a research topic, find papers that study this topic` can work better than the default when you are ranking papers.

## Limitations

- **Only the first pages are used.** A PDF whose relevant content appears later in the document can be ranked too low. Increase `NUM_PAGES` if needed, but keep `MAX_LENGTH` in mind, because everything beyond it is cut off.
- **Scanned PDFs are not supported.** If a PDF has no extractable text in the first pages (for example, it is made of images), it is skipped. Run OCR on it first.
- **Scores are relative.** Use the scores to compare PDFs for the same query. A score for one query should not be compared with a score for another query.
- **Needs a GPU.** 4-bit quantization through `bitsandbytes` is meant for NVIDIA GPUs.

